WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.380
Suppose you're writing a drawing program, with circles and rectangles.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:09.762
Every shape has a colour, and it's either filled or not.

00:00:09.762 --> 00:00:11.706
A circle also has a radius.

00:00:11.706 --> 00:00:14.370
A rectangle has a width and a height.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:16.527
So you write two classes.

00:00:16.527 --> 00:00:22.960
Each one stores a colour and a filled flag, and each one has methods to get and set them.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:24.750
Do you see the problem?

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.391
That's the same code, written twice.

00:00:28.391 --> 00:00:31.567
Add a triangle, and it's three times.

00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:35.860
And a bug you fix in one copy stays in the others.

00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:40.095
Object-oriented programming has a better answer.

00:00:40.095 --> 00:00:41.826
It's called inheritance.

00:00:41.826 --> 00:00:45.793
Welcome to Lecture 8 of Introduction to AI Programming.

00:00:46.143 --> 00:00:50.178
In this first part, we'll build a new class from an existing one,

00:00:50.178 --> 00:00:53.406
see what the new object really looks like in memory,

00:00:53.406 --> 00:00:55.703
and learn when not to inherit at all.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:57.631
Here's the plan.

00:00:57.631 --> 00:01:02.580
Write the shared part once, in a general class called GeometricObject.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:10.682
It keeps the colour and the filled flag in private fields, with two leading underscores, just like in Lecture 7.

00:01:10.682 --> 00:01:13.450
And it has getters and setters for them.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.118
The slides write these method names in camel case.

00:01:17.118 --> 00:01:22.360
We'll use Python's standard style, with lower case words joined by underscores.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.990
Its `__str__` method describes the shape.

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:29.910
Make a red shape that isn't filled, and print it.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.950
You get: colour red, and filled false.

00:01:33.734 --> 00:01:34.886
Now the circle.

00:01:34.886 --> 00:01:43.414
To make Circle a special kind of GeometricObject, put the general class in brackets after the new class's name.

00:01:43.764 --> 00:01:50.160
GeometricObject is now the superclass, also called the parent class.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.829
Circle is the subclass, or child class.

00:01:53.829 --> 00:01:57.404
We say Circle extends GeometricObject.

00:01:58.004 --> 00:02:06.164
Inside, Circle only writes what's new: the radius, and the methods that use it, like get area and get diameter.

00:02:06.514 --> 00:02:13.234
But a circle object can use every method of GeometricObject too, as if it were written in Circle.

00:02:13.584 --> 00:02:16.059
Make a circle with radius 1.5.

00:02:16.059 --> 00:02:20.101
Get colour gives green: that method is inherited.

00:02:20.101 --> 00:02:22.906
Set colour to blue: inherited too.

00:02:22.906 --> 00:02:26.784
Get radius gives 1.5: that one is Circle's own.

00:02:27.134 --> 00:02:32.566
So a subclass is usually bigger than its superclass, not smaller.

00:02:32.566 --> 00:02:36.494
It has everything the superclass has, and more.

00:02:37.267 --> 00:02:40.925
Let's look at the first line of Circle's `__init__`.

00:02:40.925 --> 00:02:45.427
It says `super().__init__`, with the colour and the filled flag.

00:02:45.777 --> 00:02:51.234
`super` means: the superclass of the class I'm written in.

00:02:51.234 --> 00:02:57.537
So this line runs GeometricObject's `__init__`, on the same object.

00:02:58.137 --> 00:02:59.546
Watch it happen.

00:02:59.546 --> 00:03:01.483
We call Circle of 1.5.

00:03:01.483 --> 00:03:10.817
Python makes one new, empty object, and runs Circle's `__init__` on it, with self pointing to that object.

00:03:11.167 --> 00:03:15.985
The first line calls GeometricObject's `__init__`, with the same self.

00:03:15.985 --> 00:03:20.527
It puts the colour and the filled flag on the object, and returns.

00:03:20.877 --> 00:03:24.757
Then Circle's `__init__` carries on, and adds the radius.

00:03:25.107 --> 00:03:29.312
Two initializers ran, but there is only one object.

00:03:29.312 --> 00:03:34.507
The superclass sets up its part, and the subclass adds its own.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.191
You can check this yourself.

00:03:37.191 --> 00:03:40.500
The function `vars` shows an object's attributes.

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:43.350
Three fields, all on one object.

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:47.490
There's no separate GeometricObject hidden inside it.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.334
The names look odd because of the two underscores.

00:03:51.334 --> 00:03:56.156
Inside a class, Python adds the class name to a private field's name.

00:03:56.156 --> 00:03:59.440
This is the name mangling you met in Lecture 7.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.411
And notice what's missing: the methods.

00:04:02.411 --> 00:04:04.630
They're not stored on the object.

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:06.310
They live in the classes.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:09.090
The object only knows its class.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.824
And each class knows its superclass.

00:04:11.824 --> 00:04:17.140
In part three, we'll see how Python uses these links to find a method.

00:04:17.933 --> 00:04:19.337
Time to predict.

00:04:19.337 --> 00:04:23.284
Here's class A, which sets a field named `i`.

00:04:23.284 --> 00:04:29.950
Class B extends A, and has its own `__init__`, which sets a field named `j`.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:32.493
We make an object of class B.

00:04:33.043 --> 00:04:34.846
Pause the video.

00:04:34.846 --> 00:04:38.563
What is `b.j`? And what is `b.i`?

00:04:39.113 --> 00:04:40.261
`b.j` is 0.

00:04:40.261 --> 00:04:46.833
But `b.i` is an error: the B object has no attribute named `i`.

00:04:47.183 --> 00:04:47.542
Why?

00:04:47.542 --> 00:04:52.833
B has its own `__init__`, so making a B runs only that one.

00:04:52.833 --> 00:04:58.303
A's `__init__` never runs, so the field `i` is never created.

00:04:58.653 --> 00:05:06.013
The fix is one line, at the start of B's `__init__`: `super().__init__`.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:13.000
This mistake is common, and it's sneaky, because the error shows up far away from the cause.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:16.343
Here, a subclass forgets `super().__init__`.

00:05:16.343 --> 00:05:18.452
Creating the object works fine.

00:05:18.452 --> 00:05:25.390
The error comes later, when an inherited method like get colour looks for a field that was never made.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:31.820
So make `super().__init__` the first line of every subclass `__init__`.

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:34.100
Here's a second trap.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:41.620
A subclass method tries to read the superclass's private field directly, with `self.__colour`.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:48.810
Inside this class, Python mangles that name with this class's name, so it looks for a field that doesn't exist.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:53.719
Private fields are private to the class, not to the family.

00:05:53.719 --> 00:05:56.037
Use the public getter instead.

00:05:56.037 --> 00:05:58.200
That's what getters are for.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:03.098
Rectangle extends GeometricObject in exactly the same way.

00:06:03.098 --> 00:06:07.760
It calls `super().__init__` first, then adds a width and a height.

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:14.967
One note: on the slides, set height assigns the old height back to itself, and ignores its argument.

00:06:14.967 --> 00:06:16.750
Here it uses the argument.

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:19.262
Now test both classes.

00:06:19.262 --> 00:06:25.649
The radius is 1.5, the area is about 7.07, and the diameter is 3.

00:06:25.649 --> 00:06:30.660
The rectangle's area is 8, and its perimeter is 12.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:34.216
But look at the first line for each shape.

00:06:34.216 --> 00:06:39.025
Printing the object says nothing about its radius, or its size.

00:06:39.025 --> 00:06:39.330
Why?

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:45.314
Neither subclass has its own `__str__` yet, so both use the one they inherited.

00:06:45.314 --> 00:06:47.240
In part two, we'll fix that.

00:06:48.034 --> 00:06:51.314
One last question: when should you inherit?

00:06:51.664 --> 00:06:55.602
Inheritance models an is-a relationship.

00:06:55.602 --> 00:06:58.654
A circle is a geometric object.

00:06:58.654 --> 00:07:05.744
So before you write class X extends Y, say the sentence: every X is a Y.

00:07:06.344 --> 00:07:11.321
If it's false, don't inherit, even if the classes share some fields.

00:07:11.321 --> 00:07:15.127
A tree and a person both have a height and a weight.

00:07:15.127 --> 00:07:17.104
But a tree is not a person.

00:07:17.454 --> 00:07:22.174
If Tree extended Person, every tree would get a get name method,

00:07:22.174 --> 00:07:26.894
and you could pass a tree to any function that expects a person.

00:07:27.244 --> 00:07:32.781
When one class just uses another object, that's a has-a relationship.

00:07:32.781 --> 00:07:34.386
A car has an engine.

00:07:34.386 --> 00:07:36.151
A course has students.

00:07:36.151 --> 00:07:40.244
Model that with a data field, not with inheritance.

00:07:41.034 --> 00:07:42.314
Let's sum up.

00:07:42.664 --> 00:07:45.326
A subclass extends a superclass.

00:07:45.326 --> 00:07:48.904
It inherits every method, and adds its own.

00:07:49.254 --> 00:07:52.983
A subclass `__init__` calls `super().__init__` first.

00:07:52.983 --> 00:07:57.134
There's one object, and both initializers put fields on it.

00:07:57.484 --> 00:08:01.324
Inherit only when the is-a sentence is true.

00:08:01.674 --> 00:08:03.165
Here's one to try.

00:08:03.165 --> 00:08:06.812
Write a class Square that extends Rectangle.

00:08:06.812 --> 00:08:13.607
A square with side 3 should have area 9, and it needs no method except `__init__`.

00:08:13.607 --> 00:08:16.674
Is a square an instance of Rectangle?

00:08:17.224 --> 00:08:23.904
In part two, we'll override methods, and meet the class that every class inherits from.

